            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>What are the best easy hikes near Scranton and Wilkes-Barre for beginners?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              High Point, New Jersey's own state high point, is one of the gentler close-in options on this
              list despite its name — a well-marked, moderate approach with a fire tower at the summit,
              roughly 70 minutes from Scranton.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>Do I need a parking pass for hiking trails near Scranton or Wilkes-Barre?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              New Jersey state park trailheads (Worthington and Stokes State Forest, High Point State Park)
              typically charge a seasonal day-use fee. New York's Catskill Park trailheads are generally
              free, though Slide Mountain's main lot fills early on fall weekends. Pennsylvania's own
              nearby state parks — Ricketts Glen, Hickory Run — are free as well.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>Can I hike near Scranton or Wilkes-Barre in winter?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, but conditions vary sharply by region. The Kittatinny Ridge stays walkable with basic
              traction most winters. The Catskill high peaks are a different matter — Slide and Wittenberg
              see real snowpack and require traction devices (and often snowshoes) from December through
              March, with avalanche-style wind exposure near their summits.
            </div>
          </details>
